The attached image, Figure 4, demonstrates that Nano-11 nanoparticles are actively transported by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) to the draining lymph node after intramuscular injection. Panel B shows that the percentage of Nano-11-containing myeloid cells in the lymph node peaks at day 2, then significantly decreases by day 7. Panel C indicates that migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) are the primary carriers, consistently accounting for the largest proportion of Nano-11+ cells. Panel D further reveals that MigDCs exhibit the highest mean fluorescence intensity, signifying a greater uptake of Nano-11 per cell compared to other APC subtypes, enhancing antigen targeting to these key immunopotentiating cells.